<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fakhr al-Mulk ibn Ammar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fakhr al-Mulk ibn Ammar was the last ruler of Tripoli in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> from the Banu Ammar family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a>, governing from 1099. He resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> siege begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond of Saint-Gilles</a> from his castle on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-pilgrim">Mount Pilgrim</a>. In 1108 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> to seek help from the Seljuk sultan, in vain, and in July 1109 Tripoli fell, becoming the capital of the County of Tripoli.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fakhr-al-mulk-ibn-ammar.jpg" alt="Fakhr al-Mulk ibn Ammar" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles-Alexandre Debacq · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bertrand_de_Saint-Gilles_Debacq_Alexandre-Charles_(1804-1850).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
